<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Cassius Longinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-longinus">Cassius Longinus</a> was a Roman senator and general, one of the leaders of the conspiracy that assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 44 BCE. Gathering forces in the East, he defeated the Rhodian fleet in 43 or 42 BCE and sacked the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> for refusing to support him. He took his own life after the first battle of Philippi in 42 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus.jpg" alt="Gaius Cassius Longinus" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pseudo-Corbulo_Musei_Capitolini_MC561.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the civil wars after Caesar’s murder, however, Rhodes refused to support his assassins, and in 43 BCE <b>Gaius Cassius</b> defeated its fleet, took the city and seized its ships and treasures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE, defeated Pompey at Pharsalus in 48 BCE and was made dictator for life early in 44 BCE, before senators led by Brutus and <b>Cassius</b> murdered him on the Ides of March.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Philippi, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-52" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gaius Cassius Longinus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-52">What was here in 52 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
